🔥 𝗞𝘂𝗯𝗲𝗿𝗻𝗲𝘁𝗲𝘀 𝗣𝗼𝗱 𝗶𝘀 𝗥𝗲𝗮𝗱𝘆, 𝗯𝘂𝘁 𝗥𝗲𝗾𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝘀 𝗮𝗿𝗲 𝗙𝗮𝗶𝗹𝗶𝗻𝗴 — 𝗪𝗵𝘆?

𝗜𝗻𝘁𝗲𝗿𝘃𝗶𝗲𝘄 𝗤𝘂𝗲𝘀𝘁𝗶𝗼𝗻:

👉 “Your Kubernetes Pods are Running and Ready, but users are getting intermittent "503 Service Unavailable" errors. How will you troubleshoot the issue?”

In production, I wouldn't assume that a Ready Pod guarantees the application is healthy end to end. I'd trace the request path and look for intermittent failures.

🔍 𝗠𝘆 𝗧𝗿𝗼𝘂𝗯𝗹𝗲𝘀𝗵𝗼𝗼𝘁𝗶𝗻𝗴 𝗔𝗽𝗽𝗿𝗼𝗮𝗰𝗵

1️⃣ Confirm the impact

Identify which endpoints, users, and environments are affected. Check when the errors started and whether they correlate with traffic spikes or recent deployments.

2️⃣ Check Pods and restarts

kubectl get pods -n <namespace> -o wide
kubectl describe pods -n <namespace>

Look for restarts, readiness probe failures, and unstable Pods.

3️⃣ Check Service endpoints

kubectl get svc -n <namespace>
kubectl get endpoints <service-name> -n <namespace>
kubectl get endpointslices -n <namespace>

Verify that the Service has the expected ready backends.

4️⃣ Inspect application logs

kubectl logs <pod-name> -n <namespace> --since=15m

Look for timeouts, connection errors, application exceptions, and dependency failures.

5️⃣ Investigate the Ingress or Load Balancer

Check controller logs, backend health, routing configuration, connection limits, and timeout settings.

6️⃣ Check dependencies and recent changes

Investigate database connectivity, external APIs, network policies, traffic spikes, and recent application or configuration changes.

7️⃣ Verify the fix

Test the endpoint again, monitor error rates, and confirm that requests are reaching healthy backends.

🎯 𝗞𝗲𝘆 𝗟𝗲𝗮𝗿𝗻𝗶𝗻𝗴

Running ≠ Ready ≠ Guaranteed to serve every request successfully.

Trace the complete request path:

Client → Load Balancer/Ingress → Service → Pod → Application Dependencies

Find the failing layer before making production changes.
